这不是一个关系情节。

00:03:49.300 --> 00:03:51.513
It's like dating hell return of the shirt.
这就像约会地狱衬衫的归还一样。

00:03:51.513 --> 00:04:03.686
You were like talk about do you remember because I don't where the idea originally came from of like that idea of going back to that person going back to that a person who's stuck in the back of your head and you go, wait a second.
你就像在谈论你还记得吗，因为我不知道这个想法最初是从哪里来的，就像回到那个人的想法，回到那个卡在你脑后的人，然后你走，等一下。

00:04:03.686 --> 00:04:05.109
Why don't I try that again?
我为什么不再尝试一下呢？

00:04:05.109 --> 00:04:13.331
Like, was that from, I forget if that was from your life or that was the room, just that specific thing of, I'm going to excavate back and find this person.
就像，那是来自，我忘记那是来自你的生活还是那个房间，只是那个特定的东西，我要挖掘回来并找到这个人。

00:04:13.331 --> 00:04:15.017
Maybe that was the right person.
也许那是对的人。

00:04:15.017 --> 00:04:16.545
Did that come from your life?
那是来自你的生活吗？

00:04:16.545 --> 00:04:17.045
I forget.
我忘记了。

00:04:17.019 --> 00:04:28.439
I think there were like two sort of fundamental truths that we got to that I think I might have brought to the room or that we sort of like build on or maybe somebody else did.
我认为我们得出了两种基本真理，我想我可能已经把它们带到了房间里，或者我们有点喜欢以此为基础，或者也许其他人已经这样做了。

00:04:28.439 --> 00:04:36.420
I'm I'm not exactly sure, but the idea of it really being hard to break up with someone, rang, always rang very true to me.
我不太确定，但与某人分手确实很难的想法对我来说总是非常真实。

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:40.339
And I'm kind of like a path of least resistance person.
我有点像一个阻力最小的人。

00:04:40.339 --> 00:04:47.220
And so I would just sort of like, ah, it's just easier to go along with this.
所以我想，啊，这样更容易接受。

00:04:47.579 --> 00:04:53.519
And so I think like that was one of the germs of the idea of like, oh, like it's hard to break up with people.
所以我认为这就是“哦，很难与人分手”这一想法的萌芽之一。

00:04:53.519 --> 00:05:00.004
And also the idea that it's so easy look back and reminisce and be like, hey, that person was great.
还有一个想法，就是很容易回顾和回忆，然后说，嘿，那个人很棒。

00:05:00.004 --> 00:05:02.792
Like, maybe, maybe there's something there.
就像，也许，也许那里有什么东西。

00:05:02.792 --> 00:05:06.683
Like that's, you know, like, well, why didn't that work out?
就像那样，你知道，就像，好吧，为什么没有成功？

00:05:06.683 --> 00:05:16.410
And you sort of like, you know, you, you, you, you talk yourself into things, you know, and I think it's a little bit like wanting to rewrite history.
你有点喜欢，你知道，你，你，你，你说服自己去做一些事情，你知道，我认为这有点像想要重写历史。

00:05:16.410 --> 00:05:32.751
It's a little bit the romantic idea of like, I've changed, you know, and so I think it was like, I think that's what I loved about the rewatch of this episode, I think it's grounded and there's these two very universal ideas that we all fall victim to.
这有点浪漫的想法，比如，我已经改变了，你知道，所以我认为，我认为这就是我喜欢重看这一集的原因，我认为它是有根据的，而且我们都成为这两个非常普遍的想法的受害者。

00:05:32.751 --> 00:05:54.540
Yeah, it makes me think one thing about the agony of breakups and it's agonizing to be broken up with, but I have found it no less agonizing to break up with someone, especially if you care about them obviously, you've spent some significant time with them presumably, But also, like, I just don't like hurting people's feelings, right?
是的，这让我想到一件事，关于分手的痛苦，分手是痛苦的，但我发现与某人分手同样痛苦，特别是如果你显然很关心他们，你可能已经和他们一起度过了一些重要的时间，但同时，就像，我只是不喜欢伤害别人的感情，对吧？

00:05:54.540 --> 00:06:05.368
Like, I avoid it at all costs, and there's something about the inability to have hard conversations, actually makes things much worse.
就像，我不惜一切代价避免它，而无法进行艰难的对话实际上会让事情变得更糟。

00:06:05.368 --> 00:06:10.540
You end up making much more terrible decisions that hurt people.
你最终会做出更糟糕的决定，伤害别人。

00:06:10.540 --> 00:06:15.339
And I also love that idea of the kind of, like, euphoric recall of a relationship.
我也喜欢那种对一段关系的欣快回忆的想法。

00:06:15.339 --> 00:06:17.658
Like, you don't remember why you broke up with her.
比如，你不记得为什么和她分手。

00:06:17.658 --> 00:06:22.750
